\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Left: the potential $V$ against $q_j$; the minimum at $q_{0j}$ is marked ``EQUILIBRIUM IS HERE!''. Right: the same well with the \leavevmode {\color  {notesblue}blue} ``APPROXIMATE BY QUADRATIC'' curve laid over it, the \leavevmode {\color  {notesblue}blue} arrow labelling the equilibrium point, and the \leavevmode {\color  {notesgreen}green} displacement $\eta _j$ measured from $q_{0j}$ --- the only place in the lecture where the small-displacement variable is introduced graphically.\relax }}{2}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Three masses $1,2,3$ coupled by two springs, and the three normal modes: $\vec  a_1 = (1,0,-1)^{\mathsf  T}$ with frequency $\omega _1$ (outer masses move oppositely, centre mass at rest); $\vec  a_2 = (-1,2,-1)^{\mathsf  T}$ with $\omega _2$; and the uniform translation $\vec  a_3 = (1,1,1)^{\mathsf  T}$ with $\omega _3 = 0$.\relax }}{5}{figure.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Two equal masses $m$ on two rods of equal length $l$ hung from a fixed ceiling, with angles $\phi _1$, $\phi _2$ measured from the downward vertical, and the unit vectors $\hat  {\bm  {r}}_1$, $\hat  {\bm  {e}}_{\phi _1}$. The \leavevmode {\color  {notesblue}blue} axes fix the sign convention: $+x$ to the right and $-z$ \emph  {downward}, which is what makes $V = -mgl(2\qopname  \relax o{cos}\phi _1 + \qopname  \relax o{cos}\phi _2)$.\relax }}{6}{figure.caption.11}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The two normal modes of the double pendulum. Left: $\vec  a_+$, the high-frequency mode $\omega _+$, in which the two bobs move in opposite senses. Right: $\vec  a_-$, the lower-frequency mode $\omega _-$, in which they move together. In both modes $|\eta _2| = \sqrt  2\,|\eta _1| > |\eta _1|$.\relax }}{9}{figure.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The two schemes side by side. Left (QM): the initial state $\left |\psi (0)\right \rangle $ is resolved into the coefficients $\{c_\alpha \}$, each is propagated by $c_\alpha e^{-iE_\alpha t/\hbar }$, and the result is reassembled into $\left |\psi (t)\right \rangle $. Right (small osc): $\vec  \eta (0),\dot  {\vec  \eta }(0)$ are mapped to $\vec  \zeta (0),\dot  {\vec  \zeta }(0)$, propagated as $\vec  \zeta (t),\dot  {\vec  \zeta }(t)$, and mapped back.\relax }}{12}{figure.caption.22}\protected@file@percent }
